<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usambara Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Usambara Mountains are a range in north-eastern Tanzania, part of the ancient Eastern Arc Mountains. Their forests are famous for their rich wildlife and plants, including the wild African violet. They were the home of the Shambaa kingdom and, from the 1890s, a centre of German colonial plantations and missions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usambara-mountains.jpg" alt="Usambara Mountains" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Ashby · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Usambara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Usambara Mountains are the easternmost ranges of the Eastern Arc Mountains, in the Lushoto District of the Tanga Region. They are about 90 kilometres long and 30 to 50 kilometres wide, and they formed nearly two million years ago by faulting and uplifting, from Precambrian metamorphic rocks. Two sub-ranges are distinguished: the West Usambaras, which are higher, and the East Usambaras, which lie nearer the coast, receive more rainfall and are significantly smaller.</p><p>The highest point is Chambolo peak at 2,289 metres. Because there were no glaciations and the climate stayed fairly consistent, the rainforest has evolved over a long period and holds a high number of endemic species, among them the Usambara eagle-owl, the Usambara akalat, the Usambara weaver and the African violet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mountains were inhabited by Bantu, Shambaa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maasai-people">Maasai people</a>, a mix of farmers and herders. A Shambaa kingdom based at Vugha was founded by Mbegha in the first half of the 18th century. His grandson Kinyashi Muanga Ike gave it a stronger political and military structure, and under Kinyashi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimweri-ye-nyumbai">Kimweri ye Nyumbai</a> it grew to cover both the West and East Usambaras and to reach the coast and the Pangani River valley. After Kimweri died in 1862 the kingdom fell apart in a succession struggle.</p><p>In the late 19th century German colonists in the Usambara District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> brought cash crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinine">quinine</a>, and designated forests as reserves for water conservation or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. In 1882 the district's governor, Walter von Saint Paul-Illaire, sent seeds of a small herb to his father, and Hermann Wendland described the plant as Saintpaulia ionantha, the African violet.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the area became part of the British mandate territory of Tanganyika, whose administration continued to reserve and exploit the forests. Foreign-controlled logging companies carried out large-scale <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> from the 1950s, and by 2000 nearly 70 percent of the original forest cover of the East Usambaras had been lost, and over 85 percent in the West.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The range can be reached from the town of Lushoto in the west and from Amani in the east. Much of the area is farmed by subsistence farmers who depend on the forests for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, medicinal plants and fuelwood. Its watersheds supply water to much of the population of north-eastern Tanzania. Protected zones have been created in the range and expanded with help from the Tanzanian government, non-governmental organisations, research teams and donor countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The Mazumbai Forest Reserve is regarded as the last pristine tropical forest in the East Usambaras.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Usambara Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Shambaa tradition, the hunter Mbegha came to the <b>Usambara Mountains</b> around the early 18th century, settled disputes, killed wild pigs that ravaged the fields and married into local lineages.</blockquote><a href="/state/shambaa-kingdom">Shambaa Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
